// Lesson 6.3 — Jump Feel: Fall Speed, Coyote Time & the Jump Buffer
// Unit 6's first rung on the ladder. Everything here is built on the student's
// own 6.1 launch and 6.2 ground check — no field, variable or branch from the
// starter is assumed to exist.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (verified by reading the Unity project at
// AP_CS_P_Starter_Project, Unity 6000.6.0f1):
//   - Assets/Prefabs/Player.prefab is a stripped prefab. Read it, not a scene:
//     the root carries a Transform and the PlayerController MonoBehaviour and
//     NOTHING ELSE; the child `Sprite` carries the SpriteRenderer and Animator.
//     There is NO Rigidbody2D and NO Collider2D on it — the components the
//     student is told to add in 2.1 and 6.1. A student who opens the prefab and
//     finds it bare is looking at the truth, and 6.1 already warns about it.
//   - Both scenes still carry stale prefab-instance overrides pointing at
//     components that no longer exist, because the project was stripped by
//     editing YAML and never re-saved in Unity:
//       * Overworld.unity's Player instance holds `m_GravityScale: 0` for a
//         Rigidbody2D fileID that is not in the prefab any more. It is dead
//         wiring and will not come back to life when the student adds a body.
//       * Platformer.unity's Player instance holds `isPlatformer: 1` for a
//         serialized bool the stripped PlayerController no longer declares.
//         That is the pre-strip project's own scene-mode flag, and it is why
//         this lesson asks the student to check the Overworld rather than
//         asserting what its Gravity Scale says. (See the neighbouring-unit
//         note below — 4.5's header comment claims no such flag exists.)
//   - Project gravity is (0, -9.81); Fixed Timestep is 0.02, so physics runs
//     fifty times a second whatever the frame rate is. Time.time is the scene
//     clock. Both numbers are quoted in the walkthrough and both are real.
//   - Tags ship as Coin, Enemy, Axe, Spikes, Ground, GroundCheck; there is no
//     Ground LAYER, which is 6.2's premise and not this lesson's business.
//   - Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity is the Unity 6 spelling (the quick reference
//     says so and 2.2/4.1 already teach it); `velocity` does not compile.
//     Object.FindFirstObjectByType<T>() is not used anywhere on this page.
//   - 6.1's technique table offers FOUR ways to leave the ground and this page
//     is compatible with three of them. Both AddForce rows and the direct
//     linearVelocity row all presuppose a Dynamic body with Gravity Scale above
//     0 — which is precisely what the fall multiplier acts on. The fourth row,
//     MovePosition() on a Kinematic body, has no gravity and no velocity at all,
//     so the walkthrough names it out loud rather than leaving a student to tune
//     a knob that can never do anything. The launch written in the walkthrough
//     and in the reference file is 6.1's BUILT row — the impulse — under 6.1's
//     own field name (jumpForce = 7f), so the two lessons do not disagree about
//     what a jump is. 6.1 also proves that AddForce leaves linearVelocity
//     untouched until the next physics step, which is the one-frame edge case
//     the rise cut has to survive (a press and release inside a single frame).
//
// Scaffolding: GUIDED (level 2), and asks.ts agrees. Two of the four numbers
// in this lesson are TIMERS THAT REMEMBER A MOMENT, and a memory you are meant
// to forget is a shape this course has never asked for: every value the game
// has kept until now — a coin count, health, an enemy's patrol target, even the
// turret's lastAttackTime — was meant to persist. So the file's shape is given,
// the arithmetic is given, and exactly one line is a gap: the window condition
// that reads the clock backwards against the remembered moment. Everything
// around it is 3.3's subtraction, 6.1's gravity and 6.2's ground check.
//   - WALK has ONE `blank: true` line, whose `what` says what to produce and
//     whose `hint` names the reading (the Time.time row, the remembered moment,
//     the knob) without printing the expression.
//   - DECOMPOSE has SIX parts and EXACTLY ONE `blank: true` part — the timer
//     itself — with its `doc` pointer left visible, because finding the answer
//     in the reading is the skill being practised, not guessing it.
//
// The "Read Function-Level Examples" section is KEPT, not deleted — the five
// examples are the same idea in five domains that are not video games, and the
// parking-meter line prints the grace-window shape, which is the precedent 3.3
// set (its gap pointed at the quick reference's Cooldown row, which prints the
// comparator). Finding the shape in the reading is the lesson, not a leak.
//
// PROTECTED BLOCKS — answer ids are POSITIONAL (lessonId:shortCode:N:qN, see
// src/lib/formify.ts), so nothing here is reordered, renumbered, added or
// removed. All four keep their document position, their <li> count and their
// REGISTERED-field count, which is what the ids actually count:
//   socratic  "Forgiving Intent Versus Playing For Them"  6 <li> / 5 fields
//   socratic  "Map Them Before You Build Them"            5 <li> / 5 fields
//   bug-hunt  "The Mushy Jump"                            6 <li> / 5 fields
//   tinker    "Feel Portfolio"                            6 <li> / 5 fields
// The item that does not register is the one whose text ends in a full stop
// rather than a question mark (formify's isQuestion). Each block still has
// exactly one such item and it is still in the same ordinal position, so the
// five ids in every block still land on the same questions. Only wording
// changed, and only where the old text contradicted the project or the physics.
// No nested <div> inside any of the four.
//
// NEIGHBOURING-UNIT NOTE, not fixed here because it is not this file: 4.5's
// header comment asserts "There is NO isPlatformer flag anywhere in this
// project and none is mentioned." The Platformer scene does carry one — a
// stale override, targeting a field the stripped script no longer declares.
// The claim is about the script and is defensible there; it is recorded here
// because it is the same stale-override trap this page's ground truth section
// is about, and because 4.5 is the lesson that touches it.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderWalkthrough, renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The two recurring questions, applied to "feel". Level 2, so exactly one
// part is withheld — the timer. Six parts, and the split is honest at the size
// test: reading half a velocity, writing a gravity multiplier, comparing a
// clock against a remembered moment, spending it, gating a jump on two windows,
// and cutting a rise are six different searches, and "make my jump feel good"
// is none of them.
const DECOMPOSE = {
